'''Debra Anne Haaland''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|h|ɑː|l|ə|n|d}};<ref>As pronounced by Haaland in [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZnubrbQvxak her speech to the 2020 Democratic National Convention].</ref> born December 2, 1960) is an American politician who served as the 54th [[United States Secretary of the Interior|United States secretary of the interior]] from 2021 to 2025. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she served as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for [[New Mexico's 1st congressional district]] from 2019 to 2021 and was chair of the [[Democratic Party of New Mexico|New Mexico Democratic Party]] from 2015 to 2017. Haaland, a [[Native Americans in the United States|Native American]], is an enrolled member of the [[Laguna Pueblo]] tribe.

Haaland's congressional district included most of [[Albuquerque]] and most of its suburbs. Along with [[Sharice Davids]], she is one of the first two Native American women elected to the [[United States Congress|U.S. Congress]]. She is a political [[Progressivism in the United States|progressive]] who supports the [[Green New Deal]] and [[Medicare for All]].

On December 17, 2020, then-president-elect [[Joe Biden]] announced that he would nominate Haaland to serve as Secretary of the Interior. She was confirmed by the [[United States Senate|US Senate]] on March 15, 2021, by a vote of 51–40.<ref name=":6" /> Following her swearing-in on March 16, she became the first Native American to serve as a Cabinet secretary and the second to serve in the Cabinet, after Republican former [[Vice President of the United States|vice president]] and [[Kaw people|Kaw Nation]] citizen [[Charles Curtis]] (who, in 1893, also became the first Native American to serve in the US Congress).

Haaland is the Democratic nominee for [[governor of New Mexico]] in the [[2026 New Mexico gubernatorial election|2026 election]]. She will face Republican nominee [[Gregg Hull]] in the November general election.<ref name=wapogov>{{cite news |title=Former Biden official Deb Haaland wins New Mexico primary for governor |work=[[The Washington Post]] |date=June 2, 2026 |access-date=June 2, 2026 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/elections/2026/06/02/new-mexico-governor-primary-election-results-live-deb-haaland-faces-sam-bergman/}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
[[File:Debra Haaland 1978.jpg|thumb|left|upright=0.8|Haaland in the [[Highland High School (Albuquerque, New Mexico)|Highland High School]] yearbook, 1978]]
Haaland was born in [[Winslow, Arizona]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Candidate Conversation – Deb Haaland (D) |url=https://insideelections.com/news/article/candidate-conversation-deb-haaland-d |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116093043/https://insideelections.com/news/article/candidate-conversation-deb-haaland-d |archive-date=November 16, 2018 |access-date=October 27, 2018 |website=insideelections.com |publisher=Inside Elections |location=Washington DC}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Peters |first=Joey |date=April 26, 2015 |title=Haaland elected new state Democratic Party Chair |url=http://nmpoliticalreport.com/3183/haaland-elected-new-democratic-party-chair/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711230149/http://nmpoliticalreport.com/3183/haaland-elected-new-democratic-party-chair/ |archive-date=July 11, 2018 |access-date=June 6, 2018 |website=New Mexico Political Report}}</ref> She is an enrolled member of the [[Laguna Pueblo]], where she belongs to the Turquoise Clan.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Reilly |first=Katie |date=June 7, 2018 |title=This Single Mother Could Be the First Native American in Congress |url=https://time.com/5304507/deb-haaland-first-native-american-congresswoman/ |url-status=live |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200810063943/https://time.com/5304507/deb-haaland-first-native-american-congresswoman/ |archive-date=August 10, 2020 |access-date=September 9, 2020}}</ref><ref name="Griswold">{{cite web |last1=Griswold |first1=Shaun |title=Deb Haaland brings the Turquoise Clan to the DNC stage |url=https://northdakotamonitor.com/2024/08/22/deb-haaland-brings-the-turquoise-clan-to-the-dnc-stage/ |website=North Dakota Monitor |publisher=North Dakota Monitor |access-date=28 September 2026}}</ref> The Pueblo people have lived on the land that is now the state of New Mexico since the 1200s and Haaland identifies herself as a 35th-generation New Mexican.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Jenkins |first=Cameron |date=November 11, 2020 |title=Deb Haaland says 'of course' she would serve as Interior secretary under Biden |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/news/525434-deb-haaland-says-of-course-she-would-serve-as-interior-secretary-under-biden |url-status=live |access-date=November 22, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201118063916/https://thehill.com/homenews/news/525434-deb-haaland-says-of-course-she-would-serve-as-interior-secretary-under-biden |archive-date=November 18, 2020}}</ref> Her mother, Mary Toya,<ref name=alb/> a [[Native Americans in the United States|Native American woman]], served in the [[United States Navy]] and also worked in the [[Bureau of Indian Affairs]].<ref name="could make history" /><ref name=":7">{{Cite news |last=Heller |first=Karen |date=July 17, 2023 |title=Interior Secretary Deb Haaland's charged mission of healing |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/2023/07/17/deb-haaland-road-to-healing/?sfmc_id=3495398 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717155310/https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/2023/07/17/deb-haaland-road-to-healing/ |archive-date=July 17, 2023 |access-date=May 19, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> Her father, Major John David "Dutch" Haaland, a [[Norwegian Minnesotan]], was an officer in the [[United States Marine Corps]] and recipient of the [[Silver Star]] for his actions in [[Vietnam War|Vietnam]]; he was buried with full military honors at [[Arlington National Cemetery]] in 2005.<ref name="alb">{{Cite web |date=March 4, 2005 |title=Obituaries: Haaland |url=http://obits.abqjournal.com/obits/show/151921 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180622060047/http://obits.abqjournal.com/obits/show/151921 |archive-date=June 22, 2018 |access-date=June 22, 2018 |website=[[Albuquerque Journal]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ellenberg |first=Richard |date=September 30, 2018 |title=Deb Haaland Biography, Wiki, Age, Husband, Net Worth, Daughter, Family, New Mexico's 1st congressional district Democrat Nominee |url=https://globintel.com/usa/deb-haaland-biography-wiki-age-husband-net-worth-daughter-family-new-mexicos-1st-congressional-district-democrat-nominee/ |access-date=March 9, 2021 |website=Glob Intel |language=en-US |archive-date=November 9, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201109023354/https://globintel.com/usa/deb-haaland-biography-wiki-age-husband-net-worth-daughter-family-new-mexicos-1st-congressional-district-democrat-nominee/ |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Puko |first=Timothy |date=March 4, 2021 |title=Interior Nominee Haaland Wins Backing from Senate Panel |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/interior-nominee-haaland-wins-backing-from-senate-panel-11614883771 |access-date=March 10, 2021}}</ref>

As [[Military brat (U.S. subculture)|a child in a military family]], Haaland moved frequently.<ref name=":2">{{Cite magazine |last=McGrady |first=Clyde |date=September 16, 2019 |title=Haaland recalls struggles as single mom, Thanksgiving and being homeless |url=https://www.rollcall.com/2019/09/16/haaland-recalls-struggles-as-single-mom-thanksgiving-and-being-homeless/ |url-status=live |magazine=[[Roll Call (magazine)|Roll Call]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201208013015/https://www.rollcall.com/2019/09/16/haaland-recalls-struggles-as-single-mom-thanksgiving-and-being-homeless/ |archive-date=December 8, 2020 |access-date=November 23, 2020}}</ref> She attended 13 public schools across the United States before the family settled in [[Albuquerque, New Mexico]], to be close to family who also belong to the Laguna Pueblo.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Boetel |first=Ryan |date=October 10, 2020 |title='Proud progressive,' Haaland seeks 2nd term |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1505620/proud-progressive-haaland-seeks-2nd-term.html |url-status=live |access-date=November 23, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023022700/https://www.abqjournal.com/1505620/proud-progressive-haaland-seeks-2nd-term.html |archive-date=October 23, 2020}}</ref> Haaland graduated from [[Highland High School (Albuquerque, New Mexico)|Highland High School]] in Albuquerque in 1978.<ref name=":2" /><ref name=":3" /> She has two sisters, a brother, and two half-sisters.<ref name="alb" />

After high school, Haaland worked at a local bakery.<ref name=":3" /><ref name=":8" /> She struggled with [[alcoholism]] during this period, including two arrests for [[driving under the influence]], but has been [[Sobriety|sober]] since enrolling in college in 1988.<ref name=":7" /><ref name=":8" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Williamson |first=Elizabeth |date=June 2, 2021 |title=The Promise and Pressures of Deb Haaland, the First Native American Cabinet Secretary |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/02/us/politics/deb-haaland-interior.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210602111436/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/02/us/politics/deb-haaland-interior.html |archive-date=June 2, 2021 |access-date=May 19, 2024 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> She earned her [[Bachelor of Arts]] in English from the [[University of New Mexico]] in 1994.<ref name="smart">{{Cite web |title=Deb Haaland's Biography |url=https://votesmart.org/candidate/biography/149368/deb-haaland |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180622035514/https://votesmart.org/candidate/biography/149368/deb-haaland |archive-date=June 22, 2018 |access-date=June 22, 2018 |website=[[Vote Smart]]}}</ref><ref name="linked">{{Cite web |year=2018 |title=Debra Haaland |url=https://www.linkedin.com/in/debra-haaland-175a9615/ |access-date=June 22, 2018 |website=[[LinkedIn]] |archive-date=March 25, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220325013905/https://www.linkedin.com/in/debra-haaland-175a9615/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Her professors included future [[United States Poet Laureate|United States poet laureate]] [[Joy Harjo]], who published Haaland's poetry in a 1997 anthology.<ref>{{Cite AV media|date=April 28, 2021 |title=Firekeeper's Daughter: A Celebration of Indigenous Literature with Angeline Boulley & Louise Erdich |medium=Video |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M0XuKsdkJ6M  |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211213/M0XuKsdkJ6M |archive-date=December 13, 2021 |url-status=live|access-date=June 2, 2021 |time=19:43 |location=YouTube |publisher=National Congress of American Indians}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Sundeen |first=Mark |author-link=Mark Sundeen |date=March 28, 2022 |title=Deb Haaland's Long Game for Victory |url=https://www.outsideonline.com/culture/opinion/deb-haaland-interior-secretary-native-issues/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221218111540/https://www.outsideonline.com/culture/opinion/deb-haaland-interior-secretary-native-issues/ |archive-date=December 18, 2022 |access-date=May 21, 2024 |work=[[Outside (magazine)|Outside]]}}</ref>

Four days after graduation, Haaland gave birth to her child, Somáh.<ref name=":2" /> As a [[single mother]], Haaland started a [[Salsa (sauce)|salsa]] company to support herself and her child.<ref name=":4">{{Cite news |last=Heild |first=Colleen |date=October 1, 2018 |title=Haaland says she shares struggles of many in NM |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1227787/haaland-says-she-shares-struggles-of-many-in-nm.html |url-status=live |access-date=November 23, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200830204345/https://www.abqjournal.com/1227787/haaland-says-she-shares-struggles-of-many-in-nm.html |archive-date=August 30, 2020}}</ref><ref name=bloomberg>{{Cite news |last1=Dlouhy |first1=Jennifer A. |last2=Epstein |first2=Jennifer |date=December 17, 2020 |title=Biden Picks Deb Haaland to Be First Native American Secretary of Interior |language=en |work=[[Bloomberg News|Bloomberg]] |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2020-12-17/biden-taps-haaland-to-be-first-native-american-head-of-interior |access-date=December 17, 2020}}</ref> At times during this period, she has said, she did not earn enough money to afford housing and had to rely on friends for shelter.<ref name=":2" /> She says she also relied on [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program|food stamps]] at times.<ref name=":4" /> Haaland began law school at the [[UCLA School of Law|University of California, Los Angeles School of Law]] in 2000 before earning her [[Juris Doctor]] in Indian law from the [[University of New Mexico School of Law]] in 2006, but narrowly failed the [[Bar examination in the United States|bar exam]] later that year.<ref>{{cite web | title=Bioguide Search | url=https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/H001080 }}</ref><ref name="smart" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Oxford |first=Andrew |date=May 2, 2017 |title=Haaland, former Dem Party state chairwoman, running for Congress |work=[[The Santa Fe New Mexican]] |url=http://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/haaland-former-dem-party-state-chairwoman-running-for-congress/article_c99ed6c6-4b18-59bd-9b3d-a14c557bfc50.html |url-status=live |access-date=June 6, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181023074832/http://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/haaland-former-dem-party-state-chairwoman-running-for-congress/article_c99ed6c6-4b18-59bd-9b3d-a14c557bfc50.html |archive-date=October 23, 2018}}</ref><ref name=":8">{{Cite magazine |last=Cep |first=Casey |author-link=Casey Cep |date=April 29, 2024 |title=Deb Haaland Confronts the History of the Federal Agency She Leads |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2024/05/06/deb-haaland-confronts-the-history-of-the-federal-agency-she-leads |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240429105619/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2024/05/06/deb-haaland-confronts-the-history-of-the-federal-agency-she-leads |archive-date=April 29, 2024 |access-date=May 19, 2024 |magazine=[[The New Yorker]]}}</ref> While serving as Interior Secretary, Haaland was working on completing a [[master's degree]] in [[Native American studies|American Indian studies]] at the [[University of California, Los Angeles]].<ref name=":7" />

==Early career==
After law school, Haaland became a counselor for adults with [[Developmental disability|developmental disabilities]], then served as a tribal administrator and casino manager in [[San Felipe Pueblo, New Mexico|San Felipe Pueblo]].<ref name="smart" /><ref name=":8" /> She was the first woman appointed to the board of the Laguna Development Corporation, a Laguna-owned enterprise created to support the growth of the Laguna Community and its economy.<ref name=":3" /><ref name=":8" /> As chairwoman of the board, she oversaw business operations for the second-largest tribal gaming enterprise in New Mexico<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Oestreich |first=Corrine |date=April 27, 2019 |title=Interview with Deb Haaland – 2019 Gathering of Nations Pow Wow |url=https://www.powwows.com/interview-with-deb-haaland-2019-gathering-of-nations-pow-wow/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190428152303/https://www.powwows.com/interview-with-deb-haaland-2019-gathering-of-nations-pow-wow/ |archive-date=April 28, 2019 |access-date=November 23, 2020 |website=powwows.com}}</ref> and successfully advocated for the corporation to create policies and commitments to earth-friendly business practices.<ref name=":5" />

Haaland was New Mexico's vote director for Native Americans in [[Barack Obama]]'s [[Barack Obama presidential campaign, 2012|2012 presidential reelection campaign]].<ref name="congress" /> She was the chair of the Native American Caucus of the [[Democratic Party of New Mexico]] from 2012 to 2013.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Dunlap |first=Susan |date=May 27, 2020 |title=NY Times highlights Congresswoman Deb Haaland |url=https://nmpoliticalreport.com/2020/05/27/ny-times-highlights-congresswoman-deb-haaland/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201130121010/https://nmpoliticalreport.com/2020/05/27/ny-times-highlights-congresswoman-deb-haaland/ |archive-date=November 30, 2020 |access-date=November 22, 2020 |website=NM Political Report}}</ref> She ran for [[Lieutenant Governor of New Mexico]] in [[New Mexico gubernatorial election, 2014|2014]].<ref name="could make history">{{Cite news |date=November 28, 2012 |title=Debra Haaland Could Make History as Lt. Gov. of NM – IndianCountryToday.com |newspaper=Ict News |url=https://newsmaven.io/indiancountrytoday/archive/debra-haaland-could-make-history-as-lt-gov-of-nm-u0hzoqZzxEOQLyolDIuIfQ/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612142846/https://newsmaven.io/indiancountrytoday/archive/debra-haaland-could-make-history-as-lt-gov-of-nm-u0hzoqZzxEOQLyolDIuIfQ/ |archive-date=June 12, 2018 |access-date=June 6, 2018 |publisher=Newsmaven.io}}</ref> Her ticket, headed by then-Attorney General of New Mexico [[Gary King (politician)|Gary King]], the Democratic nominee for [[Governor of New Mexico]], lost to the Republican ticket of Governor [[Susana Martinez]] and Lieutenant Governor [[John Sanchez]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Mexico gubernatorial and lieutenant gubernatorial election, 2014 |url=https://ballotpedia.org/New_Mexico_gubernatorial_and_lieutenant_gubernatorial_election,_2014 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181113060254/https://ballotpedia.org/New_Mexico_Gubernatorial_and_Lieutenant_Gubernatorial_election,_2014 |archive-date=November 13, 2018 |access-date=September 9, 2020 |website=Ballotpedia |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Past Election Results 2014|publisher=New Mexico Secretary of State|url=https://www.sos.state.nm.us/voting-and-elections/election-results/past-election-results-2014/|access-date=March 16, 2021|language=en-US}}</ref>

Haaland was elected to a two-year term as the chair of the [[Democratic Party of New Mexico]] in April 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Terrell |first=Steve |date=April 25, 2015 |title=State Democrats elect first American Indian to lead party |url=http://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/local_news/state-democrats-elect-first-american-indian-to-lead-party/article_b2827808-b4e3-5e0f-a62f-f832134a8ddd.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116031504/http://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/local_news/state-democrats-elect-first-american-indian-to-lead-party/article_b2827808-b4e3-5e0f-a62f-f832134a8ddd.html |archive-date=November 16, 2018 |access-date=June 6, 2018 |website=[[The Santa Fe New Mexican]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Baker |first=Deborah |date=April 26, 2015 |title=Democrats elect Haaland state party chairwoman |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/575217/dems-elect-chairwoman.html |url-status=live |access-date=June 8, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612135959/https://www.abqjournal.com/575217/dems-elect-chairwoman.html |archive-date=June 12, 2018}}</ref> During her tenure, New Mexico Democrats regained control of the [[New Mexico House of Representatives]] and the office of the [[Secretary of State of New Mexico|New Mexico secretary of state]].<ref name=congress/><ref name=":0">{{Cite magazine |last=Stuart |first=Tessa |date=August 18, 2018 |title=Meet Deb Haaland, Likely to Be the First Native Woman Elected to Congress |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/deb-haaland-first-native-woman-elected-congress-712408/ |url-status=live |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201109010421/https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/deb-haaland-first-native-woman-elected-congress-712408/ |archive-date=November 9, 2020 |access-date=November 22, 2020}}</ref> Haaland has been credited with rebuilding the state party after large defeats for Democrats in New Mexico in 2014.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Simonich |first=Milan |date=November 22, 2020 |title=For all-time comebacks, Haaland is a contender |url=https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/local_news/for-all-time-comebacks-haaland-is-a-contender/article_95a3e154-28e9-11eb-bef8-33286bb2d245.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201123175648/https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/local_news/for-all-time-comebacks-haaland-is-a-contender/article_95a3e154-28e9-11eb-bef8-33286bb2d245.html |archive-date=November 23, 2020 |access-date=November 23, 2020 |website=Santa Fe New Mexican}}</ref> She raised enough money during her two-year term as chair to pay off seven years' worth of debt incurred under previous chairs.<ref name=":0" />

==US House of Representatives==
{{See also|List of Native Americans in the United States Congress}}

===Elections===

====2018====
{{See also|2018 United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico#District 1}}
[[File:Deb Haaland official portrait, 116th congress 2.jpg|thumb|Haaland's Congressional Portrait]]

After the expiration of her term as state party chair, Haaland announced her intention to run for the [[United States House of Representatives]] in {{ushr|NM|1}} in the [[United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico, 2018|2018 elections]], to succeed [[Michelle Lujan Grisham]], who was running for governor.<ref name="congress">{{Cite news |last=Boyd |first=Dan |date=May 16, 2017 |title=Former state Democratic Party chairwoman Haaland plans run for Congress |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1003900/haaland-plans-run-for-congress.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612135849/https://www.abqjournal.com/1003900/haaland-plans-run-for-congress.html |archive-date=June 12, 2018 |access-date=June 8, 2018 |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]]}}</ref> Haaland defeated [[Damon Martinez]] and [[Antoinette Sedillo Lopez]] to win the Democratic Party nomination in June 2018,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Coleman |first=Michael |date=June 5, 2018 |title=Past Democratic Party chair Haaland wins nomination |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1180950/haaland-wins-democratic-primary-in-1st-district.html |url-status=live |access-date=June 6, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612162104/https://www.abqjournal.com/1180950/haaland-wins-democratic-primary-in-1st-district.html |archive-date=June 12, 2018}}</ref> receiving 40.5% of the vote and winning every county in the district.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Mexico Election Results |url=http://electionresults.sos.state.nm.us/resultsCTY.aspx?eid=112&type=FED&rid=4526&pty=DEM&osn=190&map=CTY |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190104124404/http://electionresults.sos.state.nm.us/resultsCTY.aspx?eid=112&type=FED&rid=4526&pty=DEM&osn=190&map=CTY |archive-date=January 4, 2019 |access-date=January 4, 2019 |website=electionresults.sos.state.nm.us}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title=New Mexico Primary Election Results: First House District |work=The New York Times |date=June 11, 2018 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/new-mexico-house-district-1-primary-election |url-status=live |access-date=January 4, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190104125807/https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/new-mexico-house-district-1-primary-election |archive-date=January 4, 2019 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

Haaland campaigned as a [[Progressivism in the United States|progressive]] who supports the [[Green New Deal]] and [[Medicare for All]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Q&A: 1st Congressional District Candidate Debra Haaland |language=en-US |work=[[The Albuquerque Journal]] |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1499521/qampa-1st-congressional-district-candidate-debra-haaland.html |access-date=December 18, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Connolly |first=Griffin |date=December 18, 2020 |title=AOC praises Biden's 'historic appointment' of first Native American to lead Interior Department |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/aoc-deb-haaland-interior-secretary-b1775848.html |access-date=December 18, 2020 |language=en |newspaper=[[The Independent]]}}</ref> Her campaign was endorsed by the climate action group [[Sunrise Movement]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Movement |first=Sunrise |date=August 8, 2018 |title=Sunrise Movement Announces First Round of Endorsed Candidates |url=https://medium.com/sunrisemvmt/sunrise-movement-announces-first-round-of-endorsed-candidates-e3638d1a5e9b |access-date=May 13, 2026 |website=Sunrise Movement |language=en}}</ref>

In the November 6 general election, Haaland defeated former New Mexico state representative [[Janice Arnold-Jones]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 7, 2018 |title=Native American Democrat Debra Haaland beats GOP's Janice Arnold-Jones, earns groundbreaking US House win in New Mexico |url=https://www.apnews.com/97669d6800a94c8bb7e5830149503e69 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190104175616/https://www.apnews.com/97669d6800a94c8bb7e5830149503e69 |archive-date=January 4, 2019 |access-date=January 4, 2019 |website=AP NEWS}}</ref> receiving 59.1% of the vote and winning three of the district's five counties.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Mexico Election Results |url=http://electionresults.sos.state.nm.us/resultsCTY.aspx?type=FED&rid=4899&osn=190&map=CTY |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190104124412/http://electionresults.sos.state.nm.us/resultsCTY.aspx?type=FED&rid=4899&osn=190&map=CTY |archive-date=January 4, 2019 |access-date=January 4, 2019 |website=electionresults.sos.state.nm.us}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title=New Mexico Election Results: First House District |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/new-mexico-house-district-1 |url-status=live |access-date=January 4, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190104124045/https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/new-mexico-house-district-1 |archive-date=January 4, 2019 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Her victory was part of a sweep of New Mexico that saw Democrats win every statewide and federal office on the ballot that year, along with expanding their majority in the [[New Mexico House of Representatives]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 14, 2019 |title=2018 Election Results Statewide Summary |url=https://api.realfile.rtsclients.com/PublicFiles/ee3072ab0d43456cb15a51f7d82c77a2/1cf3f371-f6aa-4a04-baa2-cf408244ff7d/2018-General-State-Canvass.PDF|access-date=November 23, 2020 |website=New Mexico Secretary of State}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Reichbach |first=Matthew |date=November 7, 2018 |title=Democrats expand state House majority |url=https://nmpoliticalreport.com/2018/11/07/democrats-expand-house-majority/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201124060625/https://nmpoliticalreport.com/2018/11/07/democrats-expand-house-majority/ |archive-date=November 24, 2020 |access-date=November 23, 2020 |website=NM Political Report}}</ref>

====2020====
{{See also|2020 United States House of Representatives elections in New Mexico#District 1}}

In the November 3 general election, Haaland received 58.2% of the vote defeating retired police detective Michelle Garcia Holmes, who ran for [[Lieutenant Governor of New Mexico]] in the [[2018 New Mexico gubernatorial election|2018 gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Turner |first=Scott |date=October 25, 2019 |title=Garcia Holmes enters U.S. House race |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |url=https://www.abqjournal.com/1383032/garcia-holmes-enters-u-s-house-race.html |access-date=December 24, 2020}}</ref>

===Tenure===
[[File:Deb Haaland taking oath in 2019.jpg|thumb|Congresswoman Deb Haaland (D-NM), is sworn in to the House of Representatives by Speaker Nancy Pelosi, accompanied by her mother Mary Toya and her child Somáh.]]
[[File:Congresswoman Debra Anne Haaland - Pueblo Laguna - New Mexico by Shane Balkowitsch in Wet Plate Collodion 6-23-2019.jpg|thumb|A [[wet-plate collodion]] photograph of Haaland in 2019]]

With Representative [[Sharice Davids]] of [[Kansas]], a member of the [[Ho-Chunk Nation]] of [[Minnesota]], elected simultaneously, Haaland was one of the first two [[Native Americans in the United States|Native American]] women to be seated in Congress.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Reilly |first=Kate |date=November 7, 2018 |title=Democrats in Kansas, New Mexico Become First Native American Women Elected to Congress |url=https://time.com/5446593/sharice-davids-deb-haaland-first-native-american-woman-congress/ |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190112211003/http://time.com/5446593/sharice-davids-deb-haaland-first-native-american-woman-congress/ |archive-date=January 12, 2019 |url-status=live |access-date=January 3, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Moore |first=McKnna |date=June 7, 2018 |title=The U.S. Could Be Getting Its First Native American Congresswoman in November |url=http://fortune.com/2018/06/07/deb-haaland-new-mexico/ |magazine=[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180813143513/http://fortune.com/2018/06/07/deb-haaland-new-mexico/ |archive-date=August 13, 2018 |access-date=August 13, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Romero |first=Simon |date=June 6, 2018 |title=New Mexico Could Elect First Native American Woman to Congress |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/06/us/deb-haaland-new-mexico-congress.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180625220947/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/06/us/deb-haaland-new-mexico-congress.html |archive-date=June 25, 2018 |access-date=June 25, 2018 |website=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> For her swearing-in ceremony in January 2019, Haaland chose to wear traditional Pueblo dress, a necklace, and moccasins..<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mettler |first=Lyn |date=January 4, 2019 |title=Rep. Debra Haaland wears traditional Native American dress to swearing-in ceremony |work=[[NBC News]] |url=https://www.today.com/style/rep-debra-haaland-wears-traditional-native-american-dress-swearing-t146280 |url-status=live |access-date=January 9, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190109161027/https://www.today.com/style/rep-debra-haaland-wears-traditional-native-american-dress-swearing-t146280 |archive-date=January 9, 2019}}</ref>

Later that month, Haaland said that students from [[Covington Catholic High School]] had displayed "blatant hate, disrespect, and intolerance" during the [[2019 Lincoln Memorial confrontation]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=January 20, 2019 |title=Video of US teenagers taunting Native American draws fire |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-46935701 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240215180719/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-46935701 |archive-date=February 15, 2024 |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[BBC]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Gstalter |first=Morgan |date=January 19, 2019 |title=Haaland condemns students' behavior toward Native elder at Indigenous Peoples March |url=https://thehill.com/blogs/blog-briefing-room/news/426160-haaland-condemns-students-behavior-toward-native-elder-at/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190120043159/https://thehill.com/blogs/blog-briefing-room/news/426160-haaland-condemns-students-behavior-toward-native-elder-at/ |archive-date=January 20, 2019 |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref> A [[libel]] lawsuit brought by students of the school that called Haaland's words "false and defaming" was dismissed on the grounds that her statements were made in the scope of her employment as a legislator.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McKee |first=Chris |date=August 5, 2019 |title=NM Congresswoman Haaland facing lawsuit over 'Covington Boys' tweets |url=https://www.krqe.com/news/albuquerque-metro/nm-congresswoman-haaland-facing-lawsuit-over-covington-boys-tweets/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190807130551/https://www.krqe.com/news/albuquerque-metro/nm-congresswoman-haaland-facing-lawsuit-over-covington-boys-tweets/ |archive-date=August 7, 2019 |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[KRQE]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Londberg |first=Max |date=August 2, 2019 |title=Libel suit filed against Kathy Griffin, Elizabeth Warren and others who tweeted about Covington Catholic incident |url=https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/2019/08/02/covington-catholic-students-file-libel-suit-vs-journalists-celebrities/1899474001/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240205031620/https://www.cincinnati.com/story/news/2019/08/02/covington-catholic-students-file-libel-suit-vs-journalists-celebrities/1899474001/ |archive-date=February 5, 2024 |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[The Cincinnati Enquirer]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hayes |first=Peter |date=September 3, 2020 |title=Elizabeth Warren Beats Covington Students' Defamation Suit |url=https://news.bloomberglaw.com/us-law-week/elizabeth-warren-beats-covington-students-defamation-suit |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240214140500/https://news.bloomberglaw.com/us-law-week/elizabeth-warren-beats-covington-students-defamation-suit |archive-date=February 14, 2024 |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[Bloomberg Law]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Volokh |first=Eugene |author-link=Eugene Volokh |date=September 13, 2020 |title=Libel Lawsuits Against Federal Government Officials (E.g., Senator Warren or President Trump) |url=https://reason.com/volokh/2020/09/13/libel-lawsuits-against-federal-government-officials-e-g-senator-warren-or-president-trump/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201101081332/https://reason.com/volokh/2020/09/13/libel-lawsuits-against-federal-government-officials-e-g-senator-warren-or-president-trump/ |archive-date=November 1, 2020 |access-date=May 20, 2024 |work=[[Reason (magazine)|Reason]]}}</ref>

On March 7, 2019, during a debate on voting rights and campaign finance, Haaland became the first [[Native Americans in the United States|Native American woman]] to preside over the US [[United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Frazin |first=Rachel |date=March 7, 2019 |title=First Native American Congresswoman presides over House |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/433095-first-native-american-congresswoman-presides-over-house |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190308000729/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/433095-first-native-american-congresswoman-presides-over-house |archive-date=March 8, 2019 |access-date=March 8, 2019 |website=The Hill}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kasana |first=Mehreen |date=March 7, 2019 |title=Watch Deb Haaland Become The First Native American Woman To Preside Over The House |url=https://www.bustle.com/p/video-of-deb-haaland-presiding-over-the-house-is-a-profound-moment-in-history-16821714 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190327085608/https://www.bustle.com/p/video-of-deb-haaland-presiding-over-the-house-is-a-profound-moment-in-history-16821714 |archive-date=March 27, 2019 |access-date=March 8, 2019 |website=Bustle}}</ref>

During the [[116th United States Congress|116th Congress]], Haaland "co-sponsored more bills than any other freshman in Congress, and compiled one of the most liberal voting records", according to ''[[The New Yorker]]''.<ref name=":8" />

Haaland served as one of three co-chairs of [[Elizabeth Warren]]'s [[Elizabeth Warren 2020 presidential campaign|2020 presidential campaign]].<ref>{{cite web |title=What Elizabeth Warren's Campaign Cochairs Have Learned on the Trail |url=https://www.glamour.com/story/elizabeth-warren-campaign-co-chairs-interview |website=Glamour |access-date=August 26, 2021 |date=February 11, 2020}}</ref>

