==U.S. Secretary of the Interior (2021–2025)==

===Nomination and confirmation===
On December 17, 2020, incoming President [[Joe Biden]] announced that he would nominate Haaland as [[United States Secretary of the Interior|Secretary of the Interior]].<ref name="interiorwapo">{{Cite news |last1=Eilperin |first1=Juliet |last2=Grandoni |first2=Dino |title=Biden picks Rep. Deb Haaland (D-N.M.) to be first Native American interior secretary |language=en-US |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2020/12/17/deb-haaland-interior-secretary-biden/ |url-status=live |access-date=December 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217200955/https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/2020/12/17/deb-haaland-interior-secretary-biden/ |archive-date=December 17, 2020 |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> Before Biden nominated Haaland, many senior Democrats had voiced their support for her as Secretary of the Interior, including [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives|House speaker]] [[Nancy Pelosi]], [[Party leaders of the United States House of Representatives|House Majority Leader]] [[Steny Hoyer]], and Senator [[Elizabeth Warren]]. [[Sunrise Movement]], the climate-oriented political action group which supported her Congressional campaigns, continued their support by advocating for Haaland's nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mceldowney |first=Evan |date=March 15, 2021 |title=Sunrise Movement Celebrates Confirmation of Green New Deal Champion Secretary of the Interior Deb Haaland |url=https://www.sunrisemovement.org/press-releases/deb-haaland/ |access-date=May 13, 2026 |website=Sunrise Movement |language=en-US}}</ref> Republican representatives [[Don Young]] and [[Tom Cole]] (a member of the Chickasaw Nation) also expressed their support for Haaland's nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Broadwater |first1=Luke |last2=Fandos |first2=Nicholas |date=December 16, 2020 |title=Pelosi backs Haaland for interior secretary, calling her 'one of the best' members of Congress. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/16/us/pelosi-backs-haaland-for-interior-secretary-calling-her-one-of-the-best-members-of-congress.html |access-date=December 18, 2020 |website=[[The New York Times]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Zeleny |first1=Jeff |last2=Merica |first2=Dan |last3=Krieg |first3=Gregory |last4=Lee |first4=MJ |last5=Sullivan |first5=Kate |date=December 18, 2020 |title=Biden taps Deb Haaland as first Native American interior secretary |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2020/12/17/politics/deb-haaland-interior-secretary-biden/index.html |access-date=December 18, 2020 |publisher=[[CNN]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Noisecat |first=Julian Brave |author-link=Julian Brave NoiseCat|date=November 30, 2020 |title=What a Joe Biden Cabinet Pick Might Mean for Native Americans – and Democrats |url=https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2020/11/30/deb-haaland-native-americans-interior-biden-440916 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201130231208/https://www.politico.com/news/magazine/2020/11/30/deb-haaland-native-americans-interior-biden-440916 |archive-date=November 30, 2020 |access-date=November 30, 2020 |website=[[Politico]] |language=en}}</ref>

[[File:V20210318LJ-0082 (51131084310).jpg|thumb|Haaland sworn in as Secretary of the Interior by Vice President Kamala Harris.]]

On March 15, 2021, Haaland was confirmed by the Senate by a 51–40 vote, with four Republicans ([[Susan Collins]], [[Lisa Murkowski]], [[Dan Sullivan (U.S. senator)|Dan Sullivan]], [[Lindsey Graham]]) voting to confirm.<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|last=King|first=Ledyard|title=Deb Haaland makes history as first Native American Cabinet member after Senate confirmation|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2021/03/15/deb-haaland-confirmed-becomes-first-native-american-cabinet/4700097001/|access-date=March 16, 2021|website=[[USA TODAY]]|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Higgins |first=Tucker |date=March 15, 2021 |title=Deb Haaland confirmed as first Native American Cabinet secretary |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2021/03/15/deb-haaland-to-be-confirmed-as-interior-secretary.html |access-date=March 15, 2021 |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref> She is the first [[Native Americans in the United States|Native American]] [[Cabinet of the United States|Cabinet]] secretary in US history.{{efn-num|[[Charles Curtis]], who was a Kaw citizen and served as [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] between 1929 and 1933, was the first ever Native American to serve in a [[Cabinet of the United States|United States Cabinet]], but never served as a Cabinet secretary.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lakhani |first1=Nina |title=Deb Haaland confirmed as first Indigenous US cabinet secretary |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/15/deb-haaland-interior-secretary-first-indigenous-native-american-cabinet |access-date=March 16, 2021 |agency=The Guardian |date=March 15, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Chavez|first=Aliyah|title=Deb Haaland swearing in details announced|url=https://indiancountrytoday.com/news/deb-haaland-swearing-in-details-announced|access-date=March 17, 2021|newspaper=Ict News|date=March 16, 2021 |language=en}}</ref><ref name=bloomberg/>}}<ref name=bloomberg/><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rott |first=Nathan |date=December 17, 2020 |title=In Historic Move, Biden To Pick Native American Rep. Haaland As Interior Secretary |url=https://www.npr.org/2020/12/17/937259525/in-historic-move-biden-to-pick-native-american-rep-deb-haaland-as-interior-secre |access-date=December 17, 2020 |website=[[NPR]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ortiz |first=Erik |date=December 18, 2020 |title="They feel hope": Why Rep. Haaland, nominated as first Native American interior secretary, is meaningful |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/politics-news/native-americans-rep-haaland-s-nomination-interior-secretary-signals-new-n1251727 |access-date=December 19, 2020 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> Her departure from the House triggered a [[2021 New Mexico's 1st congressional district special election|special election in 2021]]. Haaland was sworn in on March 18, 2021, wearing a combination of traditional Laguna Pueblo regalia and a colorful [[ribbon work|ribbon skirt]], custom-made for her by Agnes Woodward.<ref>{{cite web |author-first1=Scott|author-last1=Stump|title=Rep. Debra Haaland wore Indigenous dress as she made Cabinet history | website=TODAY.com | date=March 19, 2021 | url=https://www.today.com/style/rep-debra-haaland-wore-indigenous-dress-she-made-cabinet-history-t212251 | access-date=March 19, 2021}}</ref> She has brought Indigenous traditions to the role, often wearing Native jewelry and decorating her office with Native art.<ref name=":7" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Secretary Deb Haaland|periodical=|publisher=U.S. Department of the Interior|url=https://www.doi.gov/secretary-deb-haaland|format=|access-date=|last=|date=March 16, 2021|language=|pages=|quote=}}</ref>

===Tenure===
[[File:Deb Haaland on 20 February 2023 (cropped).jpg|thumb|Deb Haaland on February 20, 2023]]

On her first day as secretary, Haaland met with tribal media in a press conference organized by the department and the [[Native American Journalists Association]], speaking about her intention to include the tribes as decisions that impact them are made.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hcn.org/articles/indigenous-affairs-on-day-one-haaland-addresses-indigenous-media|title=On day one, Haaland addresses Indigenous media: Tribal journalists given first opportunity to interview first Indigenous secretary of Interior|first=Graham Lee |last=Brewer|work=[[High Country News]]|date=March 18, 2021|access-date=April 1, 2023}}</ref> In April 2021, Haaland announced a new unit within [[Bureau of Indian Affairs]] that plans to tackle the decades-long crisis of missing and murdered Native Americans, saying, "We are fully committed to assisting Tribal communities with these investigations, and the MMU will leverage every resource available to be a force-multiplier in preventing these cases from becoming cold case investigations".<ref>{{cite web | url= https://www.cnn.com/2021/04/06/us/deb-haaland-native-american-missing-murdered-unit/index.html |first1=Nicole |last1=Chavez | title= Deb Haaland creates unit to investigate killings and disappearances of Indigenous people | publisher= [[CNN]] | date= April 6, 2021 | access-date= April 7, 2021}}</ref>

In May 2021, Haaland approved the new constitution of the [[Cherokee Nation]] with protections for [[Cherokee freedmen controversy|Cherokee Freedmen]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.doi.gov/pressreleases/secretary-haaland-approves-new-constitution-cherokee-nation-guaranteeing-full|title=Secretary Haaland Approves New Constitution for Cherokee Nation, Guaranteeing Full Citizenship Rights for Cherokee Freedmen|date=May 12, 2021|website= U.S. Department of the Interior |url-status=live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20231009035636/https://www.doi.gov/pressreleases/secretary-haaland-approves-new-constitution-cherokee-nation-guaranteeing-full |archive-date= October 9, 2023 }}</ref> In June 2021, Haaland announced the creation of the [[Federal Indian Boarding School Initiative]]. The initiative's goal is to investigate long-standing abuse in the now defunct [[American Indian boarding schools|residential boarding schools]] that housed Native American children under the 1819 [[Civilization Fund Act]].<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.doi.gov/pressreleases/secretary-haaland-announces-federal-indian-boarding-school-initiative|title=Secretary Haaland Announces Federal Indian Boarding School Initiative|publisher=U.S. Department of the Interior |date=June 22, 2021|access-date=June 23, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/23/us/indigenous-children-indian-civilization-act-1819.html|title=U.S. to Search Former Native American Schools for Children's Remains|last1=Hauser |last2=Grullón Paz |first1=Christine |first2=Isabella |url-access=subscription |newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|date=June 23, 2021}}</ref> Haaland attended a series of Road to Healing events to bring together survivors and their stories.<ref name=":7" /> Haaland's grandparents had also been sent to the boarding schools.<ref name=":7" /> In November 2021, Haaland ordered a task force to determine new names for the 650 places on federally owned lands that currently use the word "[[squaw]]", a derogatory term for Native American women.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.npr.org/2021/11/19/1057367325/interior-secretary-deb-haaland-moves-to-ban-the-word-squaw-from-federal-lands|title = Interior Secretary Deb Haaland moves to ban the word 'squaw' from federal lands|work = NPR|date = November 19, 2021|last1 = Chappell|first1 = Bill |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230921153508/https://www.npr.org/2021/11/19/1057367325/interior-secretary-deb-haaland-moves-to-ban-the-word-squaw-from-federal-lands |archive-date= September 21, 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.npr.org/2022/02/23/1082579779/interior-department-derogatory-term|title = The U.S. looks to replace a derogatory name used hundreds of times on federal lands |work = NPR|date = February 23, 2022|last1 = Torchinsky|first1 = Rina |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204233857/https://www.npr.org/2022/02/23/1082579779/interior-department-derogatory-term |archive-date= December 4, 2023 }}</ref>

In March 2023, Haaland announced $25 million to be dedicated to bison conservation.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Brown |first=Matthew |date=March 3, 2023 |title=US to focus bison restoration on expanding tribal herds |work=AP |url=https://apnews.com/article/bison-restoration-tribes-haaland-6f5ad9227c70377a94a2a4efc809f3d8 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230329142955/https://apnews.com/article/bison-restoration-tribes-haaland-6f5ad9227c70377a94a2a4efc809f3d8 |archive-date= March 29, 2023 }}</ref>

Haaland was involved in President Biden's designation of [[National monument (United States)|national monuments]], including [[Avi Kwa Ame National Monument|Avi Kwa Ame]], [[Baaj Nwaavjo I'tah Kukveni – Ancestral Footprints of the Grand Canyon National Monument|Baaj Nwaavjo I'tah Kukveni – Ancestral Footprints of the Grand Canyon]], and [[Carlisle Federal Indian Boarding School National Monument|Carlisle Federal Indian Boarding School National Monuments]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=September 8, 2022 |title=Interior Secretary visits possible Avi Kwa Ame national monument landscape in Nevada  |url=https://www.wilderness.org/articles/press-release/interior-secretary-visits-possible-avi-kwa-ame-national-monument-landscape-nevada |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221201061838/https://www.wilderness.org/articles/press-release/interior-secretary-visits-possible-avi-kwa-ame-national-monument-landscape-nevada |archive-date=December 1, 2022 |access-date=December 1, 2022 |website=The Wilderness Society |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=May 22, 2023 |title=Secretary Haaland Highlights Locally Led Conservation Efforts in Visit to Grand Canyon Region |url=https://www.doi.gov/pressreleases/secretary-haaland-highlights-locally-led-conservation-efforts-visit-grand-canyon |access-date=August 8, 2023 |website= |publisher=U.S. Department of the Interior |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=January 6, 2025 |title=Deb Haaland: The Impact of President Biden's Apology to Indian Country {{!}} U.S. Department of the Interior |url=https://www.doi.gov/blog/deb-haaland-impact-president-bidens-apology-indian-country |access-date=January 13, 2025 |website=www.doi.gov |language=en}}</ref> These monuments will incorporate Native participation in land management, and Haaland has used her tenure to address historical wrongs by including tribes.<ref>{{Cite news |date=October 30, 2024 |title=America's first Native American cabinet secretary says she's righting historical wrongs |url=https://www.npr.org/2024/10/30/g-s1-29217/americas-first-native-american-cabinet-secretary-says-shes-righting-historical-wrongs |work=NPR}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=January 12, 2025 |title=Biden to create new national monuments in California's desert and far north |url=https://www.latimes.com/environment/story/2025-01-12/biden-to-create-chuckwalla-national-monument-s |access-date=January 13, 2025 |website=Los Angeles Times |language=en-US}}</ref>

Haaland spoke at the [[2024 Democratic National Convention]] on August 22, 2024.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-interior-secretary-deb-haaland-secretary-of-the-interior-speak-at-2024-democratic-national-convention-2024-dnc-night-4 | title=WATCH: Interior Secretary Deb Haaland speaks at 2024 Democratic National Convention &#124; 2024 DNC Night 4 | website=[[PBS]] | date=August 22, 2024 }}</ref>

==2026 New Mexico gubernatorial campaign==
{{main|2026 New Mexico gubernatorial election}}
On January 28, 2025, Haaland announced that she would run for [[governor of New Mexico]] in the [[2026 New Mexico gubernatorial election|2026 election]], seeking to succeed term-limited governor [[Michelle Lujan Grisham]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lee |first1=Morgan |last2=Bryan |first2=Susan Montoya |title=Deb Haaland made history as Interior secretary. Now she's running for governor of New Mexico |url=https://apnews.com/article/deb-haaland-new-mexico-gubernatorial-campaign-284549dfb209b0007d0f9e9e550fb68d |access-date=January 28, 2025 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=January 28, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> She kicked off her campaign in [[Los Alamos, New Mexico|Los Alamos]] to a large audience.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://losalamosreporter.com/2025/02/25/gubernatorial-candidate-deb-haaland-pulls-large-audience-at-sundays-meet-and-greet-in-los-alamos/ | title=Gubernatorial Candidate Deb Haaland Pulls Large Audience at Sunday's Meet and Greet in los Alamos | date=February 25, 2025 }}</ref> On June 2, 2026, Haaland won the Democratic Primary for governor.<ref name=wapogov /><ref name="NYTnom">{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/06/02/us/elections/new-mexico-democrat-governor-haaland.html|title=Deb Haaland Wins Nomination in New Mexico Governor’s Race|first=Reis|last=Thebault|date=June 2, 2026}}</ref> If elected, Haaland would become the first Native American woman governor of a [[U.S. state]] in the [[history of the United States]], and the fifth person to identify as Pueblo to govern what is now the State of New Mexico, after [[Po'pay]], [[Luis Tupatu]], [[José Gonzales|José María González]], and [[Pablo Montoya]].<ref name=NYTnom /><ref name=wapogov /><ref name="Pueblo Nations">{{cite book|last=Sando|first=Joe S.|title=Pueblo Nations: Eight Centuries of Pueblo Indian History|publisher=Clear Light Publishers|location=Santa Fe, New Mexico|date=1992}}</ref><ref name="Pueblo Profiles">{{cite book|first=Joe S.|last=Sando|title=Pueblo Profiles: Cultural Identity through Centuries of Change|publisher=Clear Light Publisher|date=1998}}.</ref><ref>{{cite book|first=Maurice|last=Crandall|title=These People Have Always Been a Republic: Indigenous Electorates in the U.S.-Mexico Borderlands, 1598-1912|publisher=The University of North Carolina Press|location=Chapel Hill|date=2019}}</ref>

